/* GENERATED by tools/build.py from tools/CONTENT_SPEC.md + docs/VISUAL.md — do not edit */
window.NOEMA_AUTHORING = {"content": "# noema-lite — content specification (any subject)\n\nThis is the contract between the **study engine** (subject-agnostic) and a **subject pack**.\nClaude follows it when ingesting new sources; `tools/validate.py` enforces it; `tools/build.py` packs it.\n\n## Repository layout of one subject\n```\nlibrary/subjects/<subject-id>/          (shared library)   or   accounts/<profile>/packs/<subject-id>/   (private)\n├── subject.json      identity + settings (below)\n├── sources.json      the sources (PDFs, links…) in chronological order; maps chapter & patch files to sources\n├── sources/          the original files (PDFs…) — archived, never published to the website\n├── chapters/chNN.json   one file per chapter (schema below)\n├── patches/*.json    ADDITIVE updates from later sources (never edit a published chapter; append)\n├── media/            pictures (svg/png/jpg/webp) + media.json registry — docs/VISUAL.md\n├── coverage/*.md     coverage checklists (every heading/command/test question → section + exercise ids)\n├── authoring/        scripts used to generate the content (provenance)\n├── translations/<locale>/chNN.json   OPTIONAL: the chapters in another language (below, \"Packs in several languages\")\n└── pack.js / pack.json   GENERATED by tools/build.py (do not edit)\n```\n\n## subject.json\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"cell-biology\", \"title\": \"Cell Biology\", \"appTitle\": \"Cell Biology Quest\", \"emoji\": \"🧬\",\n  \"group\": \"life-sciences\",                   // one of library/groups.json\n  \"description\": \"One line for the picker.\", \"language\": \"en\",   // language of the material (tutor replies in it)\n  \"features\": { \"math\": false, \"code\": false },                  // math → KaTeX; code → code highlighting & 'write' tasks\n  \"hero\": { \"headline\": \"Master **Cell Biology**, one bite at a time.\", \"mantra\": \"The one-sentence mental model.\" },\n  \"searchExamples\": \"mitosis, ATP synthase, p53\",\n  \"tutor\": { \"name\": \"Brick\", \"avatar\": \"🦉\", \"domain\": \"cell biology\",\n             \"prior\": \"What the learner already knows for THIS subject.\",\n             \"examples\": \"Concrete examples to use (e.g. a liver cell under stress).\",\n             \"interviewer\": \"an examiner for a cell biology oral exam\",\n             \"simulation\": \"an experiment with an unexpected result, a patient case…\",\n             \"terminology\": \"\\\"ATP synthase\\\", \\\"G1/S checkpoint\\\"\", \"examinerRole\": \"cell biology examiner\" } }\n```\nThe learner's personal profile (\"About me\") lives in the account, not in the subject.\n`language` is the language the material is written in (BCP 47: `en`, `el`, `de`, `pt-BR` …; default `en`). Optional `i18n`:\nthe subject's name and description in other languages — see \"Packs in several languages\" at the end.\n\n## sources.json\n```json\n{ \"sources\": [ { \"id\": \"part2\", \"title\": \"…\", \"short\": \"ECB 2\", \"subtitle\": \"…\", \"pages\": \"403–569\", \"file\": \"sources/ecb_p403-569.pdf\", \"firstPage\": 403, \"url\": \"https://… (web sources)\", \"added\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\", \"emoji\": \"📘\" } ],\n  \"chapters\": { \"ch01\": \"part1\" }, \"patches\": { \"ch11_links.json\": \"part2\" } }\n```\nEvery item inherits its chapter's source; patched-in items inherit the patch's source (or carry an explicit `\"src\"`).\nThis powers the in-app Sources menu, \"✨ what's new\" and source filtering. One entry per file (each part of a split PDF is its\nown source), web page, video or conversation.\n\n**The original files travel with the pack.** A file-based source has `\"file\": \"sources/<file name>\"` and that file is in the\nsubject's `sources/` folder — exactly the file the chapters were written from (for a split PDF: the part, never also the\nunsplit original). `make_pack.py` (skill) packages them into `<id>.noema.zip` (`pack.json` + `sources/…`) and records for each\n`fileName`, `size`, `sha256`, `mime` and, for PDFs, `pageCount`; it refuses a source without its file, a file without a source,\n(any size: big files are stored in parts and joined again by the app; `scripts/split_pdf.py` only if you WANT one source per part). `firstPage` (default 1) is the page number the chapters cite for the\nfile's first page — a part that starts at page 403 of the book has `\"firstPage\": 403`, so \"σ. 410\" opens page 8 of the part.\nThe app imports the package with its files (📥, \"Create with Claude\", or the connector's `noema_finish_upload`, which asks for\nevery packaged file); files can also be attached later in the app (📎 in the source's card).\n\n## Pedagogy rules (non-negotiable)\n> **0. ADHD-friendly, everywhere — the first rule, it shapes every block, card and exercise.** Paragraphs of 1–3 short\n> sentences; **one idea per block**; a concrete example or analogy for every abstract idea; lists and tables instead of\n> long prose; a `reveal` \"think first\" prompt before the key point; a `hook` that says why the section matters; short\n> question stems; no walls of text (a block over ~60 words is split). Read every section back as a tired learner would:\n> if the eye cannot find the point in a few seconds, rewrite it.\n\n1. **Coverage**: teach AND test every concept, number, caveat, trap, worked example and test question of the sources. Repeated material is taught once (best version). Every source exercise/test question becomes ≥1 exercise with its solution as the explanation.\n2. **Diagnostic thinking first**: wherever the source describes failures, errors, anomalies, misconceptions, differential diagnoses or \"first thing to check\", build a `debug` playbook with an ordered `askYourself` list (first-person questions), an `ask` block in the section, ≥1 `scenario` and ≥1 `order` exercise. Long playbooks get a `mnemonic`.\n3. **Pitfalls / exam traps** → callout (pitfall/exam/warn) + chapter `pitfalls` entry + ≥1 exercise tagged pitfall/exam.\n4. **Co-location**: related concepts in the same section; comparisons side by side (`compare`/`table`); each exercise attached to its section; 2–4 `quick` exercises per section.\n5. **ADHD-friendly**: see rule 0 above — it applies to every rule here.\n6. **Accuracy**: faithful to the sources; correct clear errors (and say so in the coverage notes); no invented facts.\n7. Diagrams redrawn cleanly (≤70 chars wide). Explanations teach (why right + why the tempting wrong answer is wrong). Every exercise type used several times per chapter.\n8. **Visual learning (docs/VISUAL.md §5–6) — only pictures with high teaching value.** A picture is used only when it teaches something the text alone cannot (where things are, what they look like, how they connect, how a quantity behaves); **no picture is better than a decorative or weak one**, and pictures are never added to meet a count. Look for them in three places: (a) the pictures of the sources (`tools/extract_images.py`), (b) the web — high-quality, information-rich photographs and diagrams, any licence for personal study, always with the source recorded (`tools/fetch_image.py`), (c) redrawn diagrams and **function graphs** where the subject has them (`tools/svgkit.py`). Verify each picture is correct, relevant and sharp. **Every picture gets ≥ 3 exercises of ≥ 2 visual types** and a `figure`; a chapter that has pictures has ≥ 3 visual exercises and every picture exercise type ≥ 3 times (tools/pack_rules.json). A chapter without a picture that teaches has none. Enforced by `\"authoring\": {\"minVisualPerChapter\": 3, \"minExercisesPerPicture\": 3, \"minPerKind\": 3}`.\n\n## Quality rules Q1–Q8 (binding for every chapter Claude or Gemini writes)\nThe learner never reads the sources. The app is the only place they learn from, so every lesson and exercise must stand\non its own and train real understanding. Lineage stays, but as data, not as text the learner must decode.\n\n**Q1 · Self-contained.** No exercise, card, playbook or theory block points at the structure of a source: no question\nnumbers (\"Q2\", \"Round 1 Q14\", \"Exercise 3\"), no pages / paragraphs / sections (\"p. 1\", \"σ. 23\", \"§2\"), no \"the source\",\n\"the author\", \"the conversation\", \"the example dataset on p. 1\", \"in the order the source lists them\". When a source\nexample or question is needed, **write it out in full** in the exercise (`q`, `code`, a table in the theory). Where it\ncame from goes in `cite` (below) and in the chapter's `sources`; the app shows it as a small chip **after** the answer.\n- ✗ \"What was the bug in Q2?\" → ✓ the Q2 code itself in `code`, then \"Which line makes this query return duplicates?\"\n- ✗ \"Complete the Round 1 Q15 answer.\" → ✓ \"Parquet is a file format. Which three guarantees does it **not** give by itself?\"\n- ✗ \"Put the distinctions in the order the source lists them.\" → order only what has a real order (a process, a lifecycle).\n\n**Q2 · Every question of the sources, answered and checked.** List every question / problem / exercise of the sources in\nthe chapter's map (`sourceQuestions`). Each becomes ≥ 1 exercise with its full text (verbatim, or completed with what it\nsilently assumes) and `cite.sq` = its id in the map. Solve it twice, independently, and compare with each other and with\nthe source's answer; recompute numbers with a script; run code / SQL where you can. A wrong source answer is corrected,\nand the explanation says so.\n\n**Q3 · Knowledge map first** (`map/chNN.json`, below). Write it before the chapter; every exercise names its learnable\n(`lr`); every source question and every point of the pages is in it.\n\n**Q4 · From the foundations up.** Per chapter, difficulty 1 / 2 / 3 ≈ **20 / 25 / 55 %** (±5). Inside each section the\nexercises go from easy to hard. Foundations are never skipped: every learnable of the map is taught and tested, not only the\nhard ones.\n\n**Q5 · Fewer trivial exercises.** Every exercise states its kind of thinking in `think`: `recall`, `explain`, `apply`,\n`discriminate`, `analyze`, `evaluate` or `create`. Per chapter `recall` ≤ 25 % and `apply` or higher ≥ 40 %. Trivial =\nthe answer is in the prompt; the right option stands out (longest, the only one without \"always\" / \"never\"); no one would\npick the distractors (distractors must be real misconceptions); a `tf` that copies a theory sentence; a one-word answer\nthat needs no thinking. Keep a few easy ones, make most of them make the learner think.\n\n**Q6 · The hard categories, exhaustively — on top of the basics.** Every learnable the map tags with a category (below)\ngets ≥ 2 exercises of different types and its own treatment in the theory (`compare` side by side for confusables, `flow`\nfor sequences, `terms` for acronyms and nomenclature, a callout for traps and edge cases, a `mnemonic` for what is hard to\nremember).\n\n**Q7 · Theory of high value.** Each section: hook → the basic idea → the mechanism (why) → a self-contained example →\ncontrast with what it is confused with → edge cases → a `reveal` \"think first\". Every acronym is expanded at first use and\nlisted in a `terms` block. Never \"the source says\".\n\n**Q8 · Explanations that teach, answers that were checked.** `explain` says why the right answer is right **and** why the\ntempting wrong one is wrong (≥ 2 sentences); every `mcq` has `why` for every option. After writing, **solve blind**: answer\nevery exercise without looking at its key; fix or rewrite every one where you disagree with the key or found it ambiguous.\nThen **review** a sample (below).\n\n### One learnable, many facades\nSeveral exercises may test the same learnable in different types — the same fact as an `mcq`, a `tf`, a `calc`, a `cloze`.\nThis is wanted: write the learnables first, then the facades. Roughly 300 exercises per chapter is a good size; it is a\n**mild suggestion**, never a hard limit (the checker only warns).\n\n### Coverage that must hold in every chapter\n- every exercise type that fits the chapter (the map's `fits.types`) — about 3 or more each;\n- every focus that makes sense (`tags` and the map's categories);\n- `think`: recall ≤ 25 %, apply or higher ≥ 40 %;\n- difficulty ≈ 20 / 25 / 55 %;\n- most (ideally all) learning paradigms that fit (`fits.modes`) in ≥ 2 exercises each (`mode`, below).\n\n### Learning paradigms (`mode`) — with the existing exercise types\n| `mode` | what the learner does | typical types |\n|---|---|---|\n| `contradiction` | two right-looking statements that cannot both hold: which premise breaks, which principle resolves it | mcq, free |\n| `reverse` | given the answer / output, find the question / input | mcq, write |\n| `whatif` | \"what changes if X were different?\" (counterfactual) | mcq, scenario |\n| `perturb` | a small change of a parameter: predict the effect | calc, mcq |\n| `causal` | fill the missing steps of a chain of reasoning | cloze with bank, order |\n| `distinguish` | sort descriptions between 2–3 confusable concepts | bucket, odd |\n| `translate` | code ↔ words ↔ diagram ↔ formula | match, write, img_* |\n| `ambiguity` | an under-specified problem: which clarifying question first | scenario |\n| `feynman` | explain it to a beginner / a child | free (audience in the rubric) |\n| `combine` | connect two concepts from different chapters | free, mcq |\n| `dialectic` | a thesis and a critique: find the synthesis (\"when each holds\") | mcq, free |\n\n### The knowledge map — `map/chNN.json` (written before the chapter)\n`points` is the exhaustive inventory: **every** knowledge point and concept of the chapter's pages, never a selection. A\npoint an earlier step of the Roadmap already taught carries `taughtIn` and is not taught again; every other point sits in\nsome learnable's `kp`.\n```json\n{ \"format\": \"noema.map/v1\", \"chapter\": \"ch03\",\n  \"points\": [ { \"id\": \"ch03-k07\", \"text\": \"MERGE fails when several source rows match one target row\", \"level\": \"core\",\n                \"cats\": [\"pitfall\", \"edge\"], \"cite\": {\"src\": \"ecb\", \"loc\": \"p. 41\"} },\n              { \"id\": \"ch03-k08\", \"text\": \"DELETE vs TRUNCATE: TRUNCATE keeps no per-row history\", \"level\": \"foundation\",\n                \"cats\": [\"confusable\"], \"with\": [\"ch03-k09\"], \"cite\": {\"src\": \"ecb\", \"loc\": \"p. 44\"} },\n              { \"id\": \"ch03-k02\", \"text\": \"The Delta transaction log\", \"level\": \"foundation\", \"cite\": {\"src\": \"ecb\", \"loc\": \"p. 40\"},\n                \"taughtIn\": \"<earlier node or chapter id>\" } ],\n  \"learnables\": [ { \"id\": \"ch03-l01\", \"kp\": [\"ch03-k07\"], \"goal\": \"Predict and fix a MERGE that matches several rows\" } ],\n  \"fits\": { \"types\": [\"mcq\", \"tf\", \"cloze\", \"write\", \"spotbug\", \"scenario\", \"order\", \"match\"],\n            \"focus\": [\"concept\", \"syntax\", \"pitfall\", \"debug\", \"compare\"],\n            \"modes\": [\"contradiction\", \"reverse\", \"whatif\", \"causal\", \"distinguish\", \"translate\"] },\n  \"unfit\": { \"dialectic\": \"pure syntax, nothing to argue\" },\n  \"sourceQuestions\": [ { \"id\": \"ch03-q01\", \"text\": \"full text…\", \"answer\": \"checked solution…\", \"cite\": {\"src\": \"r1\", \"loc\": \"Q14\"} } ] }\n```\n- `level` ∈ foundation · core · advanced (the order of the sections follows it).\n- `cats` ⊂ pitfall · confusable · falsefriend · detail · sequence · multipart · nomenclature · acronym · formula · edge ·\n  recent · hard-understand · hard-remember; `with` names the other point(s) of a confusable / false-friend pair.\n- Every exercise type, focus tag and mode is either in `fits` or has a reason in `unfit`.\n- Steps: (1) list every point of the pages; (2) one pass per category; (3) list the source questions; (4) group the points\n  into learnables, foundation → advanced; (5) decide what fits; (6) plan the facades of every learnable.\n\n### Fields this adds to an exercise\n`lr` (the learnable it tests — required), `think` ∈ recall · explain · apply · discriminate · analyze · evaluate · create,\n`mode` (optional, the ids above), `cite` `{ \"src\": \"<source id>\", \"loc\": \"p. 41\", \"sq\": \"ch03-q01\" }` — lineage, shown only\nafter answering, never in the text; `sq` names the source question the exercise reproduces (each one needs ≥ 1).\n\n### Mix per chapter (checked)\nrecall ≤ 25 %; apply or higher (apply, discriminate, analyze, evaluate, create) ≥ 40 %; analyze / evaluate / create ≥ 3;\ndifficulty 20 / 25 / 55 % (±5); every learnable and every categorised point ≥ 2 exercises of ≥ 2 types; every fitting\ntype and focus ≥ 3, every fitting mode ≥ 2. Exercise counts are only a mild note, never an error.\n\n### The review — `review/chNN.json` (written after the blind solve)\n```json\n{ \"format\": \"noema.review/v1\", \"chapter\": \"ch03\",\n  \"rubric\": [ { \"lr\": \"ch03-l01\", \"ok\": true, \"notes\": \"distractor C was implausible — rewritten\" } ],\n  \"blind\": { \"solved\": 312, \"mismatches\": [ { \"id\": \"ch03-e044\", \"fixed\": true } ] } }\n```\n- `blind`: you answered every exercise without its key; every disagreement is listed and fixed (key corrected or the\n  exercise rewritten until unambiguous).\n- `rubric`: ≥ 15 learnables (all of them if fewer) checked for self-containment, depth, real distractors and teaching\n  explanations; whatever falls short is rewritten. If you can start a sub-agent, let one that has not read the sources do it.\n- `make_pack.py` embeds the map and a summary of the review in the chapter (`chapter.map`, `chapter.review`).\n\n## Inline text format (all text fields)\n`**bold**`, `*italic*`, `` `inline code` ``, and `\\n` for a line break. No HTML.\nMath (subjects with `features.math`): `$…$` inline, `$$…$$` display (KaTeX). Inside math write `\\cdot` / `\\times`, never a bare `*` (it would be read as italics).\n\n## Chapter object\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch05\", \"num\": 5,\n  \"title\": \"Delta Lake Internals\",               // short\n  \"subtitle\": \"How SQL commands become files + log commits\",\n  \"emoji\": \"🔺\",\n  \"sourcePages\": \"ECB σ. 23–42 · Karp 9.2, 9.5\",   // one short line for people (parts joined by \" · \")\n  \"sources\": [{\"id\": \"ecb\", \"pages\": \"σ. 23–42\"}, {\"id\": \"karp\", \"pages\": \"9.2, 9.5\"}],   // EVERY source this chapter uses (ids of sources.json) with pages / sections / video times — shown as chips that open the file there\n  \"mantra\": \"One sentence mental model for the whole chapter.\",\n  \"objectives\": [\"You can explain …\", \"You can write …\", \"You can debug …\"],     // 4–8\n  \"sections\": [ Section ],          // 6–16 sections\n  \"debug\": [ Playbook ],            // as many as the source supports (min 3)\n  \"pitfalls\": [ {\"title\": \"...\", \"text\": \"...\", \"fix\": \"...\"} ],\n  \"flashcards\": [ {\"id\": \"ch05-f001\", \"q\": \"...\", \"a\": \"...\", \"section\": \"ch05-s03\"} ],   // 20–50, crisp; id chNN-fNNN, unique (cards without one get the id of their position at build time)\n  \"exercises\": [ Exercise ]\n}\n```\n\n## Section\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"ch05-s03\", \"title\": \"Time Travel & RESTORE\", \"hook\": \"One line: why should I care?\",\n  \"blocks\": [ Block, ... ] }       // 5–20 blocks\n```\n\n## Blocks (`t` = type)\n- `{\"t\":\"p\",\"text\":\"...\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"list\",\"items\":[\"...\",\"...\"],\"ordered\":false}`\n- `{\"t\":\"code\",\"lang\":\"sql|python|bash|yaml|text\",\"code\":\"multi\\nline\",\"caption\":\"optional\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"diagram\",\"text\":\"ASCII art\\nmulti-line\",\"caption\":\"optional\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"table\",\"head\":[\"Axis\",\"Options\"],\"rows\":[[\"..\",\"..\"]],\"caption\":\"optional\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"callout\",\"kind\":\"key|pitfall|tip|exam|warn|analogy|debug|interview\",\"title\":\"...\",\"text\":\"...\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"compare\",\"items\":[{\"title\":\"Classic\",\"points\":[\"..\",\"..\"]},{\"title\":\"Serverless\",\"points\":[\"..\"]}]}`  (2–3 items)\n- `{\"t\":\"flow\",\"items\":[\"READ\",\"WRITE\",\"VALIDATE\",\"COMMIT\"],\"caption\":\"optional\"}`   (a process chain, 2–9 short steps)\n- `{\"t\":\"reveal\",\"label\":\"Think first: …?\",\"text\":\"answer revealed on click\"}`\n- `{\"t\":\"ask\",\"title\":\"Ask yourself when … \",\"questions\":[\"Am I …?\",\"Did …?\"]}`\n- `{\"t\":\"terms\",\"items\":[{\"term\":\"Principal\",\"def\":\"...\"}]}`\n- `{\"t\":\"figure\",\"media\":\"heart-frontal\",\"caption\":\"optional\"}` — an explorable picture (docs/VISUAL.md §4)\n\n## Exercise — common fields\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"ch05-e012\", \"type\": \"...\", \"section\": \"ch05-s03\", \"difficulty\": 1, \"tags\": [\"concept\"],\n  \"quick\": false, \"q\": \"Prompt (inline format allowed)\", \"code\": \"optional code shown under the prompt\",\n  \"explain\": \"Teaching explanation shown after answering.\" }\n```\n`tags` ⊂ {concept, syntax, pitfall, debug, exam, interview, calc, compare}.\nQuality fields (Q1–Q8 above): `\"lr\": \"ch05-l03\"`, `\"think\": \"apply\"`, optional `\"mode\": \"whatif\"`, optional `\"cite\": {\"src\": \"ecb\", \"loc\": \"p. 41\", \"sq\": \"ch05-q02\"}`.\nIDs: `chNN-eNNN`, unique, sequential.\n\n### Types (extra fields)\n| type | fields | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `mcq` | `options:[str]`, `answer:int` or `answer:[int,...]` + `\"multi\":true`, optional `why:[str per option]` | 3–5 options, plausible distractors; `why` strongly encouraged |\n| `tf` | `answer:true/false` | `q` is a statement. Great for myths & traps. |\n| `odd` | `options:[4 str]`, `answer:int` | \"Which one doesn't belong?\" — explain the grouping |\n| `order` | `items:[str]` in the CORRECT order | 3–8 items; engine shuffles |\n| `match` | `pairs:[[left,right],...]` | 3–6 pairs, rights must be distinct |\n| `bucket` | `buckets:[str]` (2–4), `items:[{\"text\":..,\"bucket\":idx}]` | 5–10 items |\n| `cloze` | `text` with blanks `[[answer|alt answer|alt]]`, `\"bank\":[distractors]` optional, `\"asCode\":true` for code | With `bank` → learner taps chips into blanks; without → types. First variant is canonical. Answers compared case-insensitively, whitespace-trimmed. |\n| `spotbug` | `lines:[str]` (code lines), `bugs:[line indexes, 0-based]`, `fix:\"corrected code or text\"` | learner clicks the buggy line(s) |\n| `calc` | `answer:number`, `tolerance:number`, `unit:\"tasks\"` | numeric reasoning |\n| `scenario` | `steps:[{\"prompt\":..,\"code\":opt,\"options\":[{\"text\":..,\"ok\":true/false,\"fb\":\"feedback\"}]}]` | debugging simulations: each step = \"what do you check/do next?\". 2–5 steps, exactly one ok per step, every option has `fb`. |\n| `free` | `model:\"model answer\"`, `rubric:[\"key point\",...]` | explain-in-your-own-words / interview answers. Graded by Gemini or self-check. |\n| `write` | `solution:\"code\"`, `keywords:[\"merge into\",\"when matched\",...]`, `lang` | learner writes real syntax; local check = all keywords present (case-insensitive substring) |\n| `flaw` | `lines:[str]` (≥ 3: a worked solution, argument or code), `flaws:[line indexes]`, `reasons:[str]` (2–5), `reason:int`, optional `fix`, `asCode`/`lang` for code | **find the flaw**: the learner taps the wrong line(s) AND picks why; both must be right. Distractor reasons are the tempting misreadings. |\n| `case` | `q` = the situation; `parts:[{q, type: \"mcq\"\\|\"tf\"\\|\"calc\", …that type's fields, optional explain}]` (2–4) | **case**: one realistic situation, several questions about it, each marked on its own; right only when every part is right. |\n| `predict` | `options` + `answer:int` (+ `why`), or `answer:number` + `tolerance` + `unit`; `outcome:\"what actually happens\"` | **predict before you see**: the learner commits first; `outcome` is shown after. Great for `whatif` / `perturb`. |\n| `tree` | `start:\"a\"`, `nodes:{id:{q, options:[{text, ok, fb, to?}]}}` | **decision tree**: one `ok` per node; `to` names the next node (none = the end); every wrong option's `fb` says where that branch leads. The right path must end without a loop. |\n| `inquiry` | `options:[str]` (3–5 questions, tests or experiments), `scores:[0–3 per option]` with exactly one highest, `why:[str per option]` | **best question**: which one tells you the most? Values are shown afterwards. |\n| `debate` | `q` = the claim; `sides:[{position, counters:[str] (2–4), best:int, why:[str per counter]}]` (2–3) | **debate**: the learner takes ANY side (not graded), then picks the strongest objection to it. Pairs well with `mode: \"dialectic\"`. |\n| `img_hotspot` `img_sequence` `img_reveal` `img_drag` `img_label` `img_select` `img_occlusion` | `media` + regions/targets/answer… | **visual exercises — full schema in docs/VISUAL.md** |\n\n## Debug playbook\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"ch05-d01\", \"title\": \"MERGE fails: multiple source rows matched\", \"section\": \"ch05-s08\",\n  \"symptom\": \"What the learner sees (error text / behaviour)\",\n  \"askYourself\": [\"Question 1 I ask myself first?\", \"Question 2?\", \"...\"],     // ordered, 3–9\n  \"steps\": [ {\"do\": \"Run DESCRIBE HISTORY …\", \"why\": \"…\", \"code\": \"optional\"} ],\n  \"rootCauses\": [\"…\"], \"fix\": \"…\", \"mnemonic\": \"optional short memory hook\" }\n```\n\n## Quantity guide\nChecked per chapter by `tools/pack_rules.json` (one rule list, read by `validate.py` / `make_pack.py` / `build.py` and by\n`engine/packcheck.js` for the app, the Gemini generator and the connector's `noema_finish_upload`; the steps in order:\n`docs/PIPELINES.md`). For packs with `subject.authoring.rules` ≥ 2 (written by `start_subject.py`):\n* **only what fits**: the chapter's map (`map/chNN.json` `fits`) declares the exercise types, focus tags and paradigms\n  (`mode`) that make sense for it; every other value has a reason in `unfit` (code types without `features.code`,\n  `calc` without `features.math` and the picture types without a picture are unfit by themselves);\n* **every type and focus tag that fits ≥ 3 times**, every paradigm that fits ≥ 2 times;\n* **difficulty 1 / 2 / 3 ≈ 20 / 25 / 55 %** (± 5), **recall ≤ 25 %**, apply or higher ≥ 40 %, ≥ 3 critical-thinking items;\n* **every learnable** ≥ 2 exercises in ≥ 2 types; every map point not taught earlier sits in a learnable (exhaustive).\n* **Sizes are a mild suggestion, never an error or a warning**: about 2 facades per learnable in the core set and a\n  reserve without a cap; 40–70 learnables per node give ~80–140 core and 160–280 exercises in all; above ~180 in the\n  core, think about splitting the node.\nErrors when `authoring.by` is claude or gemini, warnings for learner or import packs. Older packs without `rules` keep the\nearlier quantity rules of their own `authoring.minPerKind`, if they have one; chapters of a pack being extended\n(`unpack.py`) warn.\nAlso: ≥ 5 exercises per section, ≥ 2 quick ones each; ≥ 30% of exercises tagged `pitfall`, `debug` or `exam`.\nBe exhaustive rather than brief: depth and coverage beat polish.\n\n## Also produce\n`chNN.coverage.md`: a checklist of EVERY numbered heading / concept / command / test question in your page range →\nthe section id and exercise ids that cover it. Use it to self-audit before finishing; add anything missing.\n\n\n## Additive updates (new sources for an existing subject)\n* Never edit or delete published chapter files — progress and SRS schedules are keyed by section/exercise/flashcard ids.\n* New topics → new chapter files (next free `chNN`). Material for existing sections → a patch file in `patches/`:\n```json\n{\"patches\":[{\"chapter\":\"ch02\",\"section\":\"ch02-s05\",\"appendBlocks\":[…],\"appendExercises\":[…],\"appendFlashcards\":[…],\"appendPitfalls\":[…],\"appendDebug\":[…]}]}\n```\n  Patched exercise and flashcard ids must not collide: reserve a range per patch file (e.g. `chNN-e7NN`, `chNN-f7NN`); patch cards without an id get the next free one. Databricks: `e6NN` = visual_v1, `e7NN`–`e9NN` = the links patches.\n  Typical patch content: a `callout` (kind key/tip, title \"Level-up → …\") linking to the deeper new chapter, plus genuinely new nuances with an exercise each.\n* Register the new source in `sources.json` (append; order = chronology) and map the new chapter/patch files to it.\n* `python3 tools/build.py` validates the MERGED result; `python3 tools/db_sync.py` records the new version in the backup database.\n\n## Validation\n`python3 tools/validate.py FILE.json` (single chapter) — `python3 tools/build.py` (everything, merged with patches).\n\n## Packs in several languages (optional — pack schema v2, additive)\nA pack is written in ONE language (`subject.language`). It MAY also offer its metadata and, optionally, its chapters in\nother languages; the learner sees them when the pack has their **interface language** (or its language: `es-ES` → `es`).\nThe interface language never changes content by itself: without a translation for it the learner sees the pack's own\ntexts, with a small language tag (e.g. `EN`) beside the name. **Never required** — add translations only when the user\nasks for them (e.g. a Greek class using an English pack). Locale codes: the app's UI locales `el ru fr de it es-ES pt-PT\nsq tr ar-EG hi ja zh-Hans zh-Hant en`, or a bare language (`es`, `pt`, `ar`) that covers its regional ones.\n\n**1. Metadata** — `subject.json` → `i18n`, one object per locale with any of `title`, `appTitle`, `description`,\n`searchExamples`, `hero` {`headline`, `mantra`}, `tutor` {any of the subject's own tutor keys, e.g. `domain`, `examples`}.\nOnly fields the subject itself has; a missing field shows the source text. Shown in the subject picker, the Shelf,\nExplore, shared/public packs and on the subject's home.\n```json\n\"language\": \"en\",\n\"i18n\": { \"el\": { \"title\": \"Κυτταρική Βιολογία\", \"description\": \"Μία γραμμή για τον κατάλογο.\", \"hero\": { \"headline\": \"Κατάκτησε την **Κυτταρική Βιολογία**.\" } },\n          \"fr\": { \"title\": \"Biologie cellulaire\", \"description\": \"Une ligne pour le catalogue.\" } }\n```\n**2. Content (optional)** — `translations/<locale>/chNN.json`: the WHOLE chapter in that language, exactly as built\n(base chapter + its patches), with **the same ids, the same sections and blocks in the same order, the same exercises,\nplaybooks, flashcards and pitfalls, the same answers** (option order, answer indexes, buckets, bug lines, picture regions,\n`targets`, `media`) — only the words change (titles, texts, questions, options, explanations, cards, cloze answers).\nProgress, spaced repetition and sources are keyed by the ids, so they are shared between the languages. A chapter without\na translation is shown in the pack's language. Labels drawn on a picture come from `media/media.json` (one language): a\ntranslated `img_drag` / `img_label` / `img_select` exercise may carry its own `regions` with translated labels.\nWhen a later patch adds items to a translated chapter, add them to every `translations/<locale>/chNN.json` too (the\nvalidator refuses a translation with missing ids). Add `subject.i18n.<locale>` too, so the picker shows the translated name.\n\n**Built pack** (`tools/build.py`, `make_pack.py`): `\"v\": 2` when the pack has `subject.i18n` or translations (older apps\nignore both and show the source), and `\"translations\": { \"<locale>\": { \"chapters\": [ … ] } }` beside `\"chapters\"`.\n**Validation**: `python3 tools/validate.py subject.json --subject [--translations el,fr]` (locale codes, known fields,\nonly what the source has) and `python3 tools/validate.py translations/el/ch03.json --source ch03.json` (the same ids,\nstructure and answers as the built source chapter); both run inside `build.py` and `make_pack.py`.\n\n## Language help for learners of another language (文A bridges — optional, a sidecar)\nA pack in one language can also get **bridges**: its content words, in the meaning they have in the pack, translated into\na learner's native language, with each section's key words and a short preview. They live beside the pack\n(`bridges/core.json` + `bridges/<native>.json`), never inside it, and are made with `tools/bridge.py` — the format, the\nrules and the workflow are in docs/BRIDGE.md (in the skill: `references/BRIDGE.md`). Not part of a pack's validation;\nonly when the user asks.\n\n", "visual": "# Visual exercises — canonical schema `noema.visual/v1`\n\nImages, diagrams and interactive \"do it on the picture\" exercises, for **any** subject:\nanatomy and medicine (label the heart, find the lesion), biology (cell parts), chemistry\n(lab set-ups), geography (maps), languages (label the picture), engineering (architecture\ndiagrams), and so on.\n\nThis file is the **contract**. Content authors (Claude, when ingesting sources) follow it,\n`tools/validate.py` enforces it, `tools/build.py` packs it and the engine renders it.\nThe machine-readable version is `tools/schemas/noema.visual.v1.schema.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Media registry — `media/media.json` (one per subject)\n\n```\nlibrary/subjects/<subject-id>/media/\n├── media.json            the registry (below)\n├── heart-frontal.svg     the files: .svg (preferred for diagrams), .png, .jpg/.jpeg, .webp\n└── …\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": \"noema.media/v1\",\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"heart-frontal\",                   // [a-z0-9-]+, unique in the subject\n      \"file\": \"heart-frontal.svg\",\n      \"origin\": \"drawn\",                       // drawn | plot | source | web  (§5)\n      \"url\": \"https://…\", \"retrieved\": \"2026-10-06\",   // REQUIRED for origin \"web\": the page it came from\n      \"lowResOk\": \"reason\",                    // optional: allow a raster < 800 px (small flat diagram)\n      \"alt\": \"Frontal section of the heart with the four chambers and the great vessels\",   // REQUIRED, describes the picture\n      \"caption\": \"Blood enters the right atrium…\",  // optional, shown under figures\n      \"credit\": \"Drawn by noema-lite\",            // REQUIRED: who made it\n      \"license\": \"own\",                          // REQUIRED: own | CC0 | CC BY 4.0 | … (only reuse what the license allows)\n      \"src\": \"part1\",                            // optional: the source (sources.json id) the content comes from\n      \"w\": 800, \"h\": 520,                        // optional: auto-detected for svg/png/jpeg/webp\n      \"regions\": [ Region, … ]                   // optional: the named parts of the picture, defined ONCE here\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n* **Coordinates are image units**: the SVG `viewBox` (or the pixel size of a raster image),\n  origin top-left. The engine scales them with the picture, so they stay correct on any screen.\n* The build embeds every file into the pack (data URI), so packs stay one self-contained file\n  (website, bundle, export/import, cloud). Keep rasters ≤ 400 KB (the validator warns).\n* Diagrams that Claude draws: generate them with `tools/svgkit.py`, which writes the SVG **and**\n  the matching regions, so the clickable areas always line up with the drawing.\n\n## 2. Region\n\nA named, clickable / coverable part of a picture.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"lv\",                         // [a-z0-9_-]+, unique within the picture/exercise\n  \"shape\": \"rect\", \"x\": 410, \"y\": 250, \"w\": 120, \"h\": 90, \"rx\": 8,   // rect (rx optional)\n  // or  \"shape\": \"circle\", \"cx\": 300, \"cy\": 200, \"r\": 40\n  // or  \"shape\": \"poly\",   \"points\": [[10,10],[60,15],[40,70]]       // ≥ 3 points\n  \"label\": \"Left ventricle\",          // canonical name: the answer of drag / label / select\n  \"accept\": [\"LV\", \"left ventricle of the heart\"],    // more accepted typed answers (img_label)\n  \"options\": [\"Left ventricle\", \"Right ventricle\", \"Left atrium\"],   // img_select: this dropdown only\n  \"anchor\": [470, 295],               // where the pin / input / drop zone sits (default: centre)\n  \"q\": \"Which chamber pumps into the aorta?\",          // img_occlusion: question for this mask\n  \"note\": \"Thickest wall: it pumps into the systemic circulation.\"   // teaching note after answering\n}\n```\n\n## 3. Exercise types\n\nAll the common exercise fields apply (`id`, `type`, `section`, `difficulty`, `tags`, `quick`,\n`q`, `explain`, `src`). Visual exercises add:\n\n| field | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `media` | **required** — id in `media.json` |\n| `regions` | the regions to use; **omit** to use the picture's own `regions` from `media.json` |\n| `targets` | ids of the regions that are asked (default: all regions) |\n\n| type | label in the app | what the learner does | extra fields |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `img_hotspot` | 📍 Find it on the image | taps the place(s) on the picture | `answer: [regionId,…]` (≥1; more = tap all of them), `any: true` (one tap in any answer region is enough, e.g. “tap a vertebra”), `maskLabels: bool` (cover all regions so their labels can't be read), `why: {regionId: \"why this tempting place is wrong\"}` |\n| `img_sequence` | 🧭 Trace the path | taps parts in the right order (flow of blood, data, a reaction pathway…) | `answer: [regionId,…]` in order (2–9) |\n| `img_reveal` | 🧩 Reveal & answer | uncovers tiles one by one and answers as early as possible (fewer tiles = more XP) | `options: [str]`, `answer: int` or `[int]` + `\"multi\": true`, either `grid: [cols, rows]` (auto tiles) or tile `regions`, `start: [regionId]` (tiles open at the start) |\n| `img_drag` | 🏷️ Drag the labels | drags label chips onto the covered parts (or taps chip → taps place) | `distractors: [str]` (extra wrong labels), `mask: bool` (default true: cover the targets) |\n| `img_label` | ✏️ Label the image | types the name of each covered part | `fuzzy: bool` (default true: tolerate a small typo, the correct spelling is shown), `mask` |\n| `img_select` | 🔽 Pick the labels | chooses from a dropdown at each part | `distractors: [str]` (added to the shared list), per-region `options` override, `mask` |\n| `img_occlusion` | 🙈 Cover & recall | sees one covered part at a time (optionally its `q`), recalls, reveals, marks ✓ / ✗ | `pass: 0–1` (share of ✓ needed, default 0.8), `order: \"listed\" \\| \"random\"` |\n\nRules:\n* `answer`, `targets`, `start` refer to existing region ids.\n* drag / label / select / occlusion targets need a `label`; labels within one exercise should be distinct.\n* Every region lies inside the picture (`0 ≤ x ≤ w`, `0 ≤ y ≤ h`).\n* `explain` teaches the whole picture (why each part is where it is, and the tempting mix-ups).\n\n### Example\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"ch02-e801\", \"type\": \"img_drag\", \"section\": \"ch02-s04\", \"difficulty\": 2, \"tags\": [\"concept\"],\n  \"media\": \"heart-frontal\", \"targets\": [\"ra\", \"rv\", \"la\", \"lv\"], \"distractors\": [\"Pericardium\"],\n  \"q\": \"Drag the four chambers onto the heart.\",\n  \"explain\": \"Right side = deoxygenated blood (to the lungs), left side = oxygenated blood (to the body)…\" }\n```\n\n## 4. Theory block `figure`\n\n```json\n{ \"t\": \"figure\", \"media\": \"heart-frontal\", \"caption\": \"optional (default: the media caption)\",\n  \"regions\": \"optional — default: the picture's regions\", \"explore\": true }\n```\nRenders the picture inside a section. With regions it is **explorable**: hover / tap a part to\nsee its label and note; **🙈 Hide labels** covers every part for an instant self-test.\n\n## 5. Authoring rules (every ingestion) — mandatory\n\n**Only pictures with high teaching value** (user rule, 2026-10-21): a picture is used only when it teaches something\nthe text alone cannot; **no picture is better than a decorative or weak one**, and pictures are never added to meet a\ncount. Every time sources are turned into a subject pack (or added to one), look for such pictures in **three places**:\n\n| kind | what | how | `origin` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Pictures from the sources** | the figures, photos, scans, charts and vector diagrams inside the PDFs / files **that teach** — not every one: skip logos, decoration, stock photos, duplicates and figures the text already says fully | `tools/extract_images.py scan SOURCE.pdf OUT/` (embedded images + page renders, contact sheet) → `crop` the useful ones at 200 dpi | `source` |\n| **Pictures from the web** | photographs **and** diagrams that add information the sources lack (anatomy plates, micrographs, real apparatus, maps, real software screenshots…) — even when the sources have no pictures at all; any licence, source always recorded (§6) | `tools/find_images.py` / connector `noema_image_search` (Bing + DuckDuckGo Images = the whole web, Wikimedia Commons, Openverse, NASA, iNaturalist, Wellcome, museums) → `tools/fetch_image.py` / `noema_image_fetch` (pages and Wikimedia file pages resolved to the picture) | `web` |\n| **Drawings & graphs we make** | clean redraws of the sources’ diagrams, process flows, timelines; **function graphs** (γραφικές παραστάσεις) whenever the subject has functions, rates, distributions, kinetics… | `tools/svgkit.py` — `Diagram` and `Plot` (curves, points, intervals → regions) | `drawn` / `plot` |\n\nRules:\n1. **Every picture carries several exercises** — ≥ 3, of ≥ 2 different visual types (e.g. drag the\n   names + hotspot a reasoning question + occlusion), plus a `figure` in the section that teaches it.\n   Enforced by `\"authoring\": {\"minVisualPerChapter\": 3, \"minExercisesPerPicture\": 3}`.\n2. A chapter **with** pictures: ≥ 1 `figure`, ≥ 3 visual exercises of ≥ 2 visual types, and every picture exercise\n   type that fits ≥ 3 times (`tools/pack_rules.json`; the chapter's map declares what fits). A chapter with no picture that teaches has none —\n   that is fine and is not an error.\n3. **Information value first.** A picture earns its place only if it teaches something the text\n   alone does not (where things are, what they look like, how they connect, how a quantity behaves).\n   No decorative stock photos.\n4. **High quality.** Rasters ≥ 800 px on the long side (prefer ≥ 1200), sharp, readable labels, no\n   watermarks, no heavy JPEG artefacts; crop to the informative part. (`lowResOk: \"reason\"` only for\n   small flat diagrams that stay crisp.) Keep each file ≤ 600 KB (crop; PNG-256 for screenshots, JPEG q85 for photos).\n5. **Correct and relevant — verify before use.** Look at every picture yourself (open it), check\n   that it shows what the alt text says, that labels/numbers agree with the sources and with\n   authoritative references, and that it is about *this* topic at *this* level. Reject anything\n   doubtful. Region coordinates are checked by drawing them over the picture before publishing.\n6. Pick the exercise type by the skill: **where is it** → hotspot · **what is it** → drag / select /\n   label (easy → hard) · **in what order** → sequence · **infer from partial evidence** → reveal ·\n   **long-term recall of many parts** → occlusion. Hotspots should need reasoning (“where is the data\n   read from?”), not label-reading; use `maskLabels` when labels would give the answer away.\n7. Accessibility: meaningful `alt`, readable label sizes (≥ 14 image units at 800 wide), colour is\n   never the only cue.\n\n## 6. Where web pictures may come from (licences)\n\nnoema-lite is a **personal study app**: you may use **any** picture you find — openly licensed or not —\n**as long as its source is recorded**: `url` (the page it came from), `credit` (author / owner) and\n`license` (what the page says, e.g. \"CC BY-SA 4.0\", \"CC BY-NC 4.0\", \"All rights reserved\"). The app shows\nthis under every picture with a link back.\n\n* **Open** licences (`tools/noema_lib.OPEN_LICENSES`: own, CC0, public domain, CC BY, CC BY-SA, Apache-2.0,\n  MIT, BSD, OGL, U.S. Government works) can be shared and published freely.\n* Everything else (NC, ND, all rights reserved, unknown) is marked **`restricted`** by the build: fine for\n  your own study; the app **warns** before you make such a pack public or share it with someone, because\n  redistributing it may need the owner’s permission (not legal advice).\n* Quality and information value still come first (§5): sharp, correct, relevant, ≥ 800 px.\n\nHow to search (engine/imglib.js — one library for the app, the picture service and the connector):\n* **The whole web first**: Bing Images and DuckDuckGo Images (what a Google image search shows too) via `find_images.py`,\n  the connector's `noema_image_search`, the picture service `/api/imgsearch`, or Claude's own web search for pages with\n  figures. Then the open collections below when they fit.\n* A **page** is enough: `/api/img`, `noema_image_fetch` and `fetch_image.py` take its main picture (og:image…);\n  Wikimedia / Wikipedia file pages (any language, `#/media/File:…`, small thumbnails) become the file itself\n  (Special:FilePath / a 1600–2400 px rendering) — fetching the file page as HTML is what used to fail.\n* Personal study: any licence; the source is always recorded and non-open pictures are flagged before sharing.\n\nGood places to look:\n\n| subject area | sources |\n|---|---|\n| anatomy, medicine, biology | Wikimedia Commons (Gray’s Anatomy plates, many CC BY-SA diagrams), OpenStax (CC BY 4.0), Servier Medical Art (CC BY 4.0), NIH / NCI / CDC libraries, Open-i, textbook publishers’ figure pages (restricted) |\n| physics, chemistry, earth & space | Wikimedia Commons, NASA, NOAA, USGS, OpenStax, university lecture pages (often restricted) |\n| geography, history, art | Wikimedia Commons, Library of Congress, Smithsonian Open Access, museum collections |\n| software & data engineering | official docs and blogs (screenshots: usually restricted), open-source project docs (Apache-2.0 / MIT) |\n\n`media.json` for a web picture:\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"heart-gray\", \"file\": \"heart-gray.jpg\", \"origin\": \"web\", \"alt\": \"…\", \"credit\": \"Henry Gray (Gray’s Anatomy, 1918)\",\n  \"license\": \"Public domain\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray490.png\", \"retrieved\": \"2026-10-06\", \"regions\": [ … ] }\n```\n\n**Web pictures the app downloads (`\"fetch\": \"app\"`).** When the builder's sandbox has no internet (✨ Create\nwith Claude → *Here in noema-lite*, or a claude.ai plan without network access), the picture is not stored\nas a file: the entry has no `file` but the **direct image url** and its **pixel size**; noema-lite downloads\nand embeds it when the pack is imported (through `/api/img` if the host blocks browsers), and shows it from\nthe web until then. Regions use the `w × h` coordinates.\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"heart-front\", \"origin\": \"web\", \"fetch\": \"app\", \"url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/…/Heart_anterior.jpg\",\n  \"page\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heart_anterior.jpg\", \"retrieved\": \"2026-10-07\", \"w\": 1600, \"h\": 1200,\n  \"alt\": \"…\", \"credit\": \"…\", \"license\": \"CC BY-SA 4.0\", \"regions\": [ … ] }\n```\n"};
